\title{Keccak and Ethereum}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Ethereum's workhorse is \code{keccak256}, and it is not SHA-3. Keccak won the NIST competition in 2012, NIST changed the padding rule before standardising it as FIPS 202 in 2015, and [Ethereum](dhsorens-ethereum) had already shipped the original. The two differ in padding and produce different digests for the same input, which is a reliable source of confusion for anyone reimplementing an Ethereum client.
}

\p{
    It is used almost everywhere state is named. An address is the last 20 bytes of the Keccak digest of a public key; a mapping's storage slot is the digest of the key concatenated with the slot number; Merkle–Patricia trie nodes are addressed by the digest of their RLP encoding; function selectors and event topics are digest prefixes; and the \code{KECCAK256} opcode exposes it to contracts directly.
}

\p{
    The consensus layer chose differently. SSZ's \code{hash_tree_root} is SHA-256, so a full node computes both families — Keccak for execution state and SHA-256 for beacon state. The EVM also exposes SHA-256 and RIPEMD-160 as [precompiles](dhsorens-003V) at \code{0x02} and \code{0x03}, and BLAKE2b's compression function at \code{0x09} (EIP-152, Istanbul, December 2019, priced at one gas per round).
}

\p{
    For a prover this split matters, because the [execution state](dhsorens-0047) is the Keccak half. Keccak is the most expensive of the three to prove — [Flock](dhsorens-004H) reports 30k Keccak permutations per second per core against 42k SHA-256 compressions — though the gap per byte is narrower than the raw rates suggest, since one Keccak-256 permutation absorbs 136 bytes where a SHA-256 compression takes 64.
}
